@page "/exchart/spec"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="Charts from a Chart Spec"
          Lead="An AI draws a chart by writing a Chart Spec: JSON that names the tables a capability already made and their columns, and never holds a number. The tool answers with the chart said in words, or refuses with the path and the reason, so the model can check what it drew or correct what it wrote.">

    <DocsSection Title="Data first, then the spec">
        <p>
            The data comes from the host's own capabilities: a pricing call, a query, a file read. Each returns a
            <em>Data Handle</em>, a name the host can look its table up by, and the AI writes a spec that refers to
            those handles and their columns. The numbers never pass through the model, so it cannot round, drop or
            invent one; a spec that tries to write values in is refused (ADR-0320).
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>ChartSpec.Tool</code> is the tool definition a host hands its model, whatever the model: a name,
            <code>draw_chart</code>, a description and the JSON Schema of the spec. When the model calls it, the host
            passes the JSON and its lookup to <code>ChartSpec.Answer(json, handle =&gt; table)</code> and returns
            <code>Text</code>. An accepted spec's text is the chart's summary (ADR-0275), with how many pixels were
            thinned and how many points are missing; a refused one is one sentence, such as
            <code>series[0].values is not part of a Chart Spec</code>. <code>ChartSpec.Read</code> gives the
            <code>ChartDefinition</code> to draw. A table is any <code>IChartData</code>: <code>ChartData.Of</code>
            builds one from columns, and <code>SnapshotChartData</code> reads a Snapshot by its columns' names.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each series says its Series Kind, so a model cannot draw a rate as if it were a level by accident.
            Below, a lattice priced by the page's own capability is drawn from the spec beside it. Edit the spec and
            leave the field to see the answer change, or load the spec with numbers in it to see a refusal.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ChartSpecLattice)" Mud="typeof(ChartSpecLatticeMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/LatticeTables.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Colour, size and edges from columns">
        <p>
            An Observation's marks may take their colour and their size from columns (ADR-0320). A colour is drawn
            in nine bands of the theme's heat tokens from the lowest value to the highest, or of its diverging tokens
            alike on both sides of a <code>centre</code>, such as 0 for a P&amp;L, the centre neutral. A size makes
            each marker's area proportional to its value, which is never negative. Either always brings a key at the
            plot's right, in the saved files too; a point with no value for it is drawn as an outline, and the
            Notes say how many. The tooltip and the CSV carry both values.
        </p>
        <p>
            Edges join an Observation's nodes, each named by its key column, as the branches of a lattice or the
            links of a network (ADR-0321). An edge has no look of its own and reads only as a line between its nodes;
            an edge to a key no node carries is refused, never dropped. In C#, the same are
            <code>ColourBy = new PointColour(title, values) { Centre = 0 }</code>,
            <code>SizeBy = new PointSize(title, values)</code> and <code>Edges = [new ChartEdge(from, to)]</code> on
            the series, each node's key its point's <code>Row</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="One view of a Board">
        <p>
            A Chart Spec is also one view of an <a href="exboard">ExBoard</a>, beside grids and pivots, where its
            <code>data</code> names a Board Input declared with its columns and types instead of a Data Handle, which
            lives for one conversation (ADR-0340). <code>draw_chart</code> stays as it is, and a host that draws its
            chart with ExBoard makes the spec a Board of one view with <code>BoardSpec.OfChart</code>; the ExBoard
            page runs it.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Following the data">
        <p>
            A Snapshot never changes in place; its next version is a new one. A chart drawn from a spec follows its
            handle by being read again against the Snapshot the host now holds, and <code>SnapshotChartData.Version</code>
            names the one it was read from (ADR-0322). 
        </p>
        <p>
            A table too large to send is read through a Chart Source (<code>IChartSource</code>), which thins it where
            it lives, so only what the pixels show crosses to the chart (ADR-0344).
            <code>ChartSpec.ReadAsync(json, sources, pixels)</code> asks each series for its column, from a range at a
            number of pixels; the source answers with each pixel's first, last, lowest and highest row, what each pixel
            held, and the version it read. <code>ChartSource.Thin</code> is the reference a server's source answers
            as, and <code>ChartSource.Over</code> thins a table held in the process. The tooltip counts every row under a
            pixel, not the points sent. Two series answered at different versions are refused, and so is anything a
            thinned point cannot stand for: an observation's points, a price range's candles, a label, colour, size,
            key or edge per point, and a labels axis, each by its path.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
